//! The UART drivers' shared core: rings, marks, held requests and the serve
//! decisions, over a device trait, as a library a driver and its hosted tests
//! share.
//!
//! The library holds every decision a UART driver makes and none of its
//! access to the hardware or to the kernel. `UartDevice` is the hardware: the
//! FIFO counts, the reads and stores, the interrupt causes and the transmit
//! level. A request arrives as a `uartproto` `Frame` and its reply is built
//! in a `UartBody`; the obligation a reply is made through belongs to the
//! driver, which names it to the core by a kind and a slot. `uartcore.fake`
//! implements the device over a UART that behaves as the hardware does and
//! misbehaves one way at a time, and `uartcore.bench` is a model of a driver's
//! loop over it.
//!
//! # Modules
//!
//!   - `device`: the `UartDevice` trait and the interrupt causes;
//!   - `rx`: the receive ring and its one gap mark;
//!   - `tx`: the transmit ring, the held `Write` and the `Sync` marks;
//!   - `serve`: `Core`, the state machine over both;
//!   - `fake`, `bench`: the fake UART and the driver model, for tests.

public import uartcore.device.{EV_BREAK, EV_FRAME, EV_OVERRUN, EV_PARITY, EV_RECEIVE,
                               EV_RX, EV_TX, UartDevice}
public import uartcore.rx.{Counts, Input, Rx, RxTotals}
public import uartcore.serve.{Answered, Config, Core, KIND_OTHER, KIND_READ, KIND_SYNC,
                              KIND_WRITE,
                              MAX_HELD_READS, Served}
public import uartcore.tx.{MAX_SYNCS, SyncTake, Tx, TxConfig, TxTotals}
